+        {
+          "fact": "F5",
+          "source": "def-barrier-central-path-and-newton-residuals",
+          "source_section": "Definition",
+          "quote": "For a smooth inequality problem with the convention of [[def-inequality-constrained-program-and-kkt-geometry]], let $D\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be open and convex, let $f_0,g_1,\\ldots,g_m$ be convex $C^2$ functions on $D$, and let $A\\in\\mathbb R^{p\\times n}$ and $b\\in\\mathbb R^p$. Assume there exists $x\\in D$ with $Ax=b$ and $g_i(x)<0$ for every $i$. For $t>0$, its **log-barrier objective** is\n$$\\Psi_t(x)=t f_0(x)-\\sum_{i=1}^m\\log(-g_i(x)),\\qquad Ax=b,\\quad g_i(x)<0.$$\nAny attained minimizer is a central point. Its central multipliers are $\\lambda_i=-1/(t g_i(x))>0$. A central point and multiplier tuple satisfy $Ax=b$, $g(x)<0$, and $\\nabla f_0(x)+Dg(x)^T\\lambda+A^T\\nu=0$ for an equality multiplier $\\nu$.\n\nFor the conic model of [[def-conic-optimization-primal-dual-models]], let $s=h-Gx\\in\\operatorname{int}K$ and let $F$ be a self-concordant barrier on $K$. The conic **barrier objective** is $\\Phi_t(x)=t c^Tx+F(h-Gx)$ restricted to $Ax=b$. An attained minimizer $x_t$ satisfies\n$$t c-G^*\\nabla F(s_t)+A^Tw=0,\\qquad Ax_t=b,\\qquad s_t=h-Gx_t\\in\\operatorname{int}K$$\nfor some $w\\in\\mathbb R^p$. Its barrier multiplier is $z_t=-\\nabla F(s_t)/t$.\n\nFor a standard-form LP or convex QP with $Ax=b$, $x\\ge0$, and objective $f_0(x)=\\tfrac12x^TQx+c^Tx$ ($Q=0$ for an LP), define the primal-dual residuals\n$$r_d=Qx+c-A^Ty-s,\\qquad r_p=Ax-b,\\qquad r_c=Xs-\\sigma\\mu\\mathbf1,$$\nwhere $X=\\operatorname{diag}(x)$, $S=\\operatorname{diag}(s)$, $\\mu=x^Ts/n$, and $0<\\sigma<1$. For $x,s>0$, the modified KKT Newton direction solves\n$$\\begin{bmatrix}Q&-A^T&-I\\\\ A&0&0\\\\ S&0&X\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\Delta x\\\\\\Delta y\\\\\\Delta s\\end{bmatrix}=-\\begin{bmatrix}r_d\\\\r_p\\\\r_c\\end{bmatrix}.$$\n\nFor orthant, Lorentz, or positive-semidefinite cone factors, the analogous conic residuals are $r_p=Ax-b$, $r_d=A^Ty-G^*z-c$, and $r_c=s\\circ z-\\sigma\\mu e$. These factors are self-dual under their standard Euclidean or trace pairings by [[lem-nonnegative-orthant-log-barrier]], [[lem-lorentz-cone-log-barrier]], and [[lem-psd-cone-self-dual-logdet-barrier]], so $s$ and $z$ occupy the same factor spaces. For the orthant, $\\circ$ is coordinatewise multiplication and $e=\\mathbf1$. The Lorentz Jordan product is $(t,u)\\circ(q,v)=(tq+u^Tv,tv+qu)$ with $e=(1,0)$; for symmetric matrices $X\\circ S=(XS+SX)/2$ with $e=I$. In each factor, pairing $s\\circ z$ with $e$ gives $\\langle s,z\\rangle$: this is the coordinate sum for the orthant, the first coordinate for the Lorentz product, and $\\operatorname{tr}((XS+SX)/2)=\\operatorname{tr}(XS)$ for symmetric matrices by the finite-index trace identity. In each product cone, $\\mu=\\langle s,z\\rangle/\\nu$, where $\\nu$ is the sum of the factor barrier parameters. The linearized slack is $\\Delta s=-G\\Delta x$.\n\nResidual tolerances describe a candidate unless an independent argument also verifies feasibility and a primal-dual gap. A finite iteration or factorization budget that returns a candidate, failure, or inconclusive status is not itself an optimality certificate.",
